issue.body:
There are a few pages on pdfa.org where we define a categorical list of things on a single page - https://pdfa.org/extensions/ is the main one I'm thinking about at this point, but there might be others.

Our product documentation wants to refer to things in that list, and we can do so because each individual section has an ID (eg https://pdfa.org/extensions/#pdf-2-0-hash-algorithm-extensions-sha-3-and-shake256). But that ID is not exposed on the website, you have to dig into the HTML source code to find it. It would be nice if you could have a "pop-up §" when you roll over the headings, in the same way as (for example) https://docs.asciidoctor.org/asciidoctor/latest/syntax-highlighting/rouge/  - roll over any heading on that page, and just to the left the "§" appears with the internal link to that section.

This is easy to do in theory (although I gather it's WP so maybe not in practice :smile:), but to make this change I tested the following and it works well:

1. Add the following to the stylesheet
```css
 a.anchor {
  display: none;
  position: absolute;
  margin-left: -0.75em;
  width: 0.75em;
  color: red;
}
a.anchor::before {
  content: "§";
}
:hover > a.anchor {
  display: block
}
```

2. Change the generated HTML, so instead of
```html
<h3 id="pdf-2-0-digital-signature-extensions">
 PDF 2.0 Digital Signature extensions
</h3>
```
you have
```html
<h3 id="pdf-2-0-digital-signature-extensions">
 <a class="anchor" href="#pdf-2-0-digital-signature-extensions"></a>
 PDF 2.0 Digital Signature extensions
</h3>
``` 
As an alternative to changing the HTML, you could also do this for every heading level with an ID by inserting this script run on any page after loading:
```js
document.querySelectorAll(":is(h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)[id]").forEach((e) => {
   let a = document.createElement("a");
   a.classList.add("anchor");
   a.href = "#" + e.id;
   e.insertBefore(a, e.firstChild);
});
```

comments.body:
Following from:
1. Mike's comment in the thread that was a pre-cursor to this discussion;
2. my explorations into what it takes to upgrade a PDF/UA-1 document to PDF/UA-2;
3. the desire to have PDF Assoc. documents (particularly those concerning PDF 2.0) presented as conforming to PDF/UA-2 and WTPDF;

I been working further on this (earlier version of the) Brotli report, as an example document.
Using the BFO's **octopdf** reporting tool to identify issues (however small) I now have a PDF 2.0 version that validates for all of: PDF/UA-2, WTPDF (Accessibility), WTPDF (Reuse), PDF/A-4. Here's a [link to the octopdf report](https://octopdf.com/?id=202609160185dcf7c96eb32f8c77a4b9e1935cb97c4964acd4cb59cc23089425), which justifies this claim.

I'm happy to discuss the issues that were identified, and how they need to be fixed for PDF 2.0, with anyone interested.

Strawman draft wording:

All PDF annotations require an entry called **Rect**, an upright bounding box in default user space that fully encloses the annotation (see ISO 32000, Table 166). Six annotation subtypes also define an optional entry called **QuadPoints**, which specifies a set of smaller rectangles within the **Rect** bounding box to more precisely define related content areas. The six annotation subtypes supporting **QuadPoints** are _Highlight_ (PDF 1.3), _Link_ (PDF 1.6), _Redact_ (PDF 1.7), _Squiggly_ (PDF 1.4), _StrikeOut_ (PDF 1.3), and _Underline_ (PDF 1.3). 

**QuadPoints** allows a single annotation to accurately define areas of content where the maximal bounding box would be inappropriate - for example, content that crosses multiple lines, content that spans multiple columns on a page, or content that is rotated. Old PDF creation software that doesn't leverage **QuadPoints** will produce either multiple unrelated annotations or an annotation with an inappropriately large, overlapping bounding box.  PDF viewing software that does not support **QuadPoints** is outdated and makes navigation and interaction confusing and difficult when the bounding box does not match the visual appearance when maximal bounding boxes overlap.

- {SVG picture of both a **Rect** and **QuadPoints** boxes spanning lines (upright) - similar to the example in https://pdf-issues.pdfa.org/32000-2-2020/clause14.html#H14.8.4.7.3}
- {SVG picture of both a **Rect** and **QuadPoints** boxes spanning columns (upright) - i.e. the bounding box gets very large and covers way too much}
- {SVG picture of a **Rect** and **QuadPoints** boxes for rotated content - like ISO 32000 Figure 84 but with **Rect** added}
